<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Talmud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Babylonian Talmud, Talmud Bavli, Gemara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Talmud is a central text of Rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and a primary source of Jewish law and theology, consisting of the Mishnah and its commentaries, the Gemara, written in a combination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. In 63 tractates it records the teachings and disagreements of thousands of rabbis on law, ethics, philosophy, customs, history and folklore. The earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Talmud was compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> in the late fourth or early fifth century; the more extensive and authoritative Babylonian Talmud, traditionally compiled by Rav Ashi and Ravina II around 500 CE, more likely dates from the middle of the sixth century. In Rabbinic Judaism it is second in authority only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talmud.jpg" alt="Talmud" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LGLou · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talmud-Druck_von_Daniel_Bomberg_und_Ambrosius_Froben.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Generations of rabbis in the academies of the land of Israel and Babylonia discussed and expanded it in the Gemara; together the Mishnah and Gemara form the <b>Talmud</b>, of which the Babylonian Talmud, completed around 600, became the authoritative work of Jewish law and learning.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Jewish scholars in Palestine and Babylonia wrote the <b>Talmuds</b> largely in Aramaic.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Rabbinic literature continued in Hebrew: the Mishnah, the commentaries of the Midrash, and the prayers of the synagogue, while the <b>Talmud</b> mixed Hebrew with Aramaic.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Kept to few occupations, many lent money at interest, which the Church forbade Christians to do; others traded, and the commentary of Rashi of Troyes (1040–1105) is printed in every edition of the <b>Talmud</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Seder the <b>Talmud</b> records a dispute between the schools of Hillel and Shammai: Shammai's school held that only the first psalm should be read before the meal, Hillel's the first two, the rest following the Grace After Meals, and Jewish law follows Hillel.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hallel">Hallel</a></li><li><blockquote>It is thought to rest on a teaching of the <b>Talmud</b>: two angels, one good and one evil, walk home with a person from the synagogue on Friday night.</blockquote><a href="/songs/shalom-aleichem">Shalom Aleichem</a></li></ul></section>
